<h1>Run Dekopon on a Mac</h1>
<p><a href="/deploy/kubernetes/">Kubernetes</a> is the preferred deployment; this path is for people moving from OpenClaw or Hermes.</p>
<p>Twelve steps end with a Slack agent on an always-on Mac that reviews pull requests for you and a colleague.</p>
<p>The <a href="{{ deploy }}#mac">core deploy notes ↗</a> give the reason behind each account, file mode and start order.</p>
</header>
<section id="shapes">
<h2>Run both daemons as you, or as two hidden accounts</h2>
<p>Each daemon trusts a file only when its own UID owns it, so the shape decides who edits:</p>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Shape comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Shape</th>
<th scope="col">Runs as</th>
<th scope="col">Edits</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">One account</th>
<td data-label="Runs as">You, as LaunchAgents from Homebrew, while you're logged in</td>
<td data-label="Edits">In a folder you own, then a restart</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Separate accounts</th>
<td data-label="Runs as"><code>_dekopon_broker</code> and <code>_dekopon_gateway</code>, as LaunchDaemons from a root-owned archive</td>
<td data-label="Edits">Through <code>sudo</code>, like every check and log read</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>With one account, the gateway and anything else running as you can read the GitHub token; Cedar and the broker still decide what the agent does with it.</p>
<p>Neither shape opens an inbound port: Slack Socket Mode is an outbound WebSocket, model and GitHub calls are outbound HTTPS, and the daemons talk over a local Unix socket.</p>
</section>
